# Simple Harmonic Motion: The Physics of Oscillation

> A pendulum swings, a guitar string hums, a suspension bridge shivers
> in the wind — all are oscillations. The simplest, purest kind is
> **simple harmonic motion (SHM)**: to-and-fro movement where the
> restoring force grows in exact proportion to the displacement.

## 1. What Makes Motion Harmonic?

[definition]
Motion is **simple harmonic** when the restoring force (or
acceleration) is directly proportional to the displacement from the
mean position and always directed towards it:
$F = -kx$, the minus sign marking the pull back towards the centre.
[/definition]

Because the force grows with distance, the motion is fastest at the
centre (equilibrium) and momentarily zero at the extremes — the
**amplitude** $A$. One full to-and-fro trip is one **oscillation**;
oscillations per second is the **frequency** $f$ (hertz), and the
period is $T = 1/f$.

## 2. The Equations of SHM

For displacement measured from the mean position, with $\omega = 2\pi f$:

@math label="eq:shm-displacement"
x = A\sin(\omega t)
@/math

@math label="eq:shm-period-spring"
T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{m}{k}}
@/math

[important]
The period of an ideal spring–mass system depends only on the mass and
the spring's stiffness — **not** on how far you pull it. A stiffer
spring (larger $k$) oscillates faster; a heavier mass oscillates
slower.
[/important]

## 3. Energy: Sloshing Between Kinetic and Potential

In SHM, energy swings between kinetic energy (maximum at the centre)
and potential energy (maximum at the extremes), while the total stays
constant:

@math label="eq:shm-energy"
E = \frac{1}{2}kA^2 = \text{constant}
@/math

| Position | Speed | Kinetic energy | Potential energy |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Mean position ($x = 0$) | Maximum | $\frac{1}{2}kA^2$ | 0 |
| Extreme ($x = A$) | Zero | 0 | $\frac{1}{2}kA^2$ |
| Any point $x$ | varies | $E - \frac{1}{2}kx^2$ | $\frac{1}{2}kx^2$ |

@chart bar title="Energy of a pendulum across one quarter swing"
labels:
Extreme
Half-way
Mean position

data:
