@/chart

## 4. The Simple Pendulum

For a small bob on a light string of length $l$ (swings under ~15°),
the period is:

@math label="eq:pendulum-period"
T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{l}{g}}
@/math

[tip]
The pendulum's period does **not** depend on the bob's mass or the
swing size — only on length and gravity. This is why a pendulum clock
keeps steady time: doubling the length makes the period √2 times
longer, and a pendulum taken to the Moon (g about 1/6) runs slow by
a factor of √6.
[/tip]

## 5. Damped and Forced Oscillations

Real oscillations die out because friction drains energy each cycle —
this is **damping**. The amplitude decays over time: a swinging door
closer, a car's shock absorbers, and the fading ring of a struck bell
are all damped oscillators.

Feed an oscillator a periodic push instead, and you get **forced
oscillations**. When the driving frequency matches the system's
natural frequency, amplitude explodes — **resonance**.

[warning]
Resonance is powerful and sometimes destructive. Soldiers break step
crossing bridges; the Tacoma Narrows bridge (1940) twisted itself
apart when wind-driven oscillations locked onto its natural
frequencies. Earthquake-safe buildings are designed so their natural
frequencies stay far from typical quake frequencies.
[/warning]

## 6. Resonance We Live By

Resonance is not always an enemy — technology leans on it:

- **Radio tuning** — the receiver's circuit is tuned to resonate with
  one station's frequency, amplifying it above all others.
- **MRI machines** — hydrogen nuclei resonate at specific radio
  frequencies in a magnetic field.
- **Musical instruments** — an instrument's body resonates with the
  string or air column, projecting sound loudly.
- **Swings** — small pushes timed to the swing's natural period build
  a large amplitude effortlessly.

## 7. From SHM to Waves

Every wave is born from oscillation. Connect many SHM oscillators in a
chain and the disturbance travels — a **wave**. This is the bridge to
the next lessons: a vibrating string is SHM; the ripple running down
it is a wave with speed $v = f\lambda$.

| Concept | Symbol | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Amplitude | $A$ | Maximum displacement |
| Frequency | $f$ | Oscillations per second (Hz) |
| Period | $T$ | Time per oscillation; $T = 1/f$ |
| Angular frequency | $\omega$ | $2\pi f$ (rad/s) |

[summary]
SHM is oscillation under a restoring force $F = -kx$. The period of a
spring–mass system is $2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$ and of a simple pendulum
$2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$ — independent of amplitude in both cases. Energy
sloshes between kinetic and potential forms while the total
$\frac{1}{2}kA^2$ stays fixed. Friction damps real oscillations;
driving a system at its natural frequency produces resonance, which
can amplify — or destroy.
[/summary]
